  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nA cold front moved across the Iowa border shortly after noon today, \nkicking off a period of cold advection that will result in near to \nbelow normal temperatures from Thursday through the weekend. Water \nvapor imagery highlights a zone of subsidence and drying immediately \nbehind the front, which has resulted in a wedge of mostly sunny \nskies between the frontal boundary and the cyclonic flow stratocu \nfield to the north. That cyclonic flow statocu field gets here \novernight and remains until the arrival of the surface highs starts \nto clear it out Friday. It will not be cloudy the whole time, with \ntransient holes moving about the cloud deck, but otherwise, tonight \nthrough Thursday night will be your classic fall weather with cold \nnorth winds and stratocu filled skies. As for whether or not we'll \nsee precipitation with this setup, that looks tough to do given the \nshallow depth of the cloud bearing layer, but the best chance comes \ntonight. RAP soundings show the deepest cloud layers occurring \novernight (upwards of 4k feet in western WI), with a strong \nshortwave dropping down off of Lake Superior in the northerly flow. \nThis will open the door for some shower activity overnight, mainly \neast of I-35. Any precip would be brief/light, but the deeper into \nthe night precip falls, the greater the chance you may get your \nfirst glimpse of snowflakes falling this season, but this is \ndefinitely not the snow storm you are looking for.\n\nFor Friday through the weekend, a blocking pattern at h5 will keep \nthe surface high parked over the upper MS Valley. We'll have light \nwinds and mostly clear skies, which will allow for a very gradual \nmodification of the airmass through the weekend, with highs in the \nupper 30s on Friday heading for the low to mid 40s by Sunday (which \nis where our normals are at the moment). To go with this will be our \nfirst truly chilly mornings of the fall, with Saturday morning \nlooking the coldest, with some lows in the teens possible. For the \nTwin Cities, we haven't seen a low even approach 20 since a 23 back \non April 7th, so this will coldest we've been in 7 months. But that \ncold won't last long...\n\nNext week, the upper ridge will move off to the east as troughing \ndevelops over western North America. It could be quite breezy Monday \nthrough Wednesday next week as a strong pressure gradient develops \nbetween low pressure over the Rockies and a high across the eastern \nseaboard. Exactly how the pressure gradient sets up will determine \njust how windy we'll be next week, but our current forecast from the \nNBM is likely a bit underdone on winds next week given model spread \nthis far out. These southerly winds also bring a quick end to our \nrun of cooler weather, with highs back into the 50s by Tuesday and \nWednesday, with some 60s not out of the question as well. Still \nmissing from the forecast early next week are rain chances, but that \nstill looks to change Thursday as the first of the waves from the \nwestern trough arrives. Although when/where any potential \nprecipitation tracks with this is still quite uncertain, it will \nstill be plenty warm enough for all rain.",
